    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">The crack in the statute</p>
        <h2>Humane slaughter already has a rule. Zabiha is the exception.</h2>
        <p>The Humane Methods of Slaughter Act, 7 U.S.C. § 1902, tells livestock plants to render cattle, calves, horses, mules, sheep, swine, and other covered livestock insensible to pain before they are shackled, hoisted, cast, or cut. That is the Western standard: one blow, a shot, electricity, or another method that is rapid and effective.</p>
<p>Section 1902(b) then steps aside for ritual slaughter — Jewish or any other religious faith — where consciousness is supposed to be lost by bleeding the brain after the simultaneous severance of the carotid arteries with a sharp instrument. Poultry, which is most of the cheap meat case, sits largely outside that livestock statute. The religious cut is not the baseline. It is a carve-out, and a narrow one.</p>
<p>Europe has already argued this in public. Denmark and Sweden require stunning. Flanders and Wallonia require it too, and the Court of Justice of the European Union held that EU law does not stop a member state from imposing stunning in the name of animal welfare. The United States does not have to be the jurisdiction that treats a conscious cut as more civilized than a stun.</p>
<p>European Food Safety Authority opinions on welfare at slaughter treat the cutting of a conscious animal as a serious welfare harm. Stunning is the scientific baseline. A marketing line about mercy does not outrank that.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">04</p>
        <h2>“Haram” is a verdict, not a finding.</h2>
        <p>The pitch says meat that is not zabiha is typically non-halal, including meat from ordinary grocery stores, and that Muslims must seek certified zabiha or they are eating what Islamic law forbids.</p>
<p>Haram means forbidden under Islamic law. It does not mean dirty, uninspected, or nutritionally other. And Islamic law is not one voice. Quran 5:5 makes the food of the People of the Book lawful for Muslims, which is why plenty of Muslims in this country eat ordinary supermarket meat without a zabiha stamp. The certifiers paper over that argument and sell the strict answer as the only answer.</p>
<p>We reject the strict answer. We also reject the “modest” one, in which everyone else must still label, segregate, and pay so the strict answer can have a parallel meat economy.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <h2>A private table is not the demand.</h2>
        <p>Nobody on this page is raiding a kitchen. The demand we are refusing is public. School lunches that drop every non-halal option. Hospital trays. Prison kitchens. Municipal bids. Packing plants retooled so a religious certifier stands over the line and invoices the packer for permission.</p>
<p>That is not a personal diet. That is a second food code. It sorts American meat into permitted and forbidden, then asks the school district to serve only the permitted.</p>
<p>The sister page, nozabihahalal.com, takes the sticker apart. islamophobe.me takes the legal system under both of them apart. The cut, the certificate, and the law are one project: make Islamic rules the house rules.</p>
        <p><a href="https://nozabihahalal.com/">Continue to nozabihahalal.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sand">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">The line in the sand</p>
        <h2>Muslim practices that put Islamic slaughter law over American animal-welfare law, and over equal public food service, are not compatible with Western values in the United States.</h2>
        <div class="prose"><p>They never will be. Repetition will not naturalize a conscious throat-cut. A friendlier logo will not make a religious permission slip into a civil standard. Guilt will not make a public cafeteria into a halal kitchen.</p>
<p>A person may believe it and may keep a private table. The public table does not convert. Equal protection does not mean every doctrine gets to rewrite the kill floor.</p>
<p>This is not a brief for harming anyone. It is a brief against pretending the rite is a civil right the rest of the country must fund, stock, and teach as normal.</p></div>
      </div>
